    h1: 'Turf cleaning in Briarcliffe Acres',
    intro: `Briarcliffe Acres is 0.64 square miles of oceanfront, the smallest and wealthiest town in our area: median household income $156,250, 94.1 percent owner-occupied, under a maritime canopy protected since 1973. Live oak and yaupon shade every acre lot; salt aerosol hits the front row off 3,500 feet of Atlantic frontage. A tree ordinance means we work around root plates.`,
    question: 'Who cleans artificial turf in Briarcliffe Acres?',
    answer: `TIMELESS Turf Restoration cleans and restores artificial turf and backyard putting greens across Briarcliffe Acres, working around the protected live oak canopy. Owner-operated by Brian, 13 years in turf. Essential Clean from $199, Premium Restoration with algae and salt treatment from $299, priced by square foot.`,
    image: { src: '/assets/img/putting-green-fire-pit-stone-wall.jpg', alt: 'Backyard putting green beside a stone wall and fire pit' },
    blocks: [
      { h2: 'Shade and salt, in the same yard', html: `<p>Briarcliffe Acres sits inside a mature maritime forest, a Bird Sanctuary since 1973, and that canopy is why natural grass fails on these acre lots. Turf under oak gets moisture and shade — what algae needs — while the front row takes direct salt aerosol off 3,500 feet of Atlantic frontage. Often the same lot needs both: <a href="/services/algae-and-mold-removal/">algae removal</a> for a shaded corner, <a href="/services/hard-water-and-stain-removal/">salt removal</a> for the dulled front section.</p>` },
      { h2: `A backyard green for the town's actual buyer`, html: `<p>Median age 60.7, median household income $156,250, an acre of shaded ground and usually no architectural committee to clear it with — the Briarcliffe Acres Association manages common areas, not individual lots. A green under live oaks needs more attention than one in the open: catkins and leaf drop in spring, acorns in fall, shade slowing even drying. <a href="/services/putting-green-restoration/">Putting green restoration</a>, cleared, top-dressed, brushed and rolled.</p>` },
      { h2: 'The tree ordinance, and what it means for us', html: `<p>The town's Public Tree Ordinance #2022.04 protects the canopy that makes Briarcliffe Acres what it is, and any work near a live oak's root plate is a real consideration, not a formality. Cleaning and restoring turf doesn't disturb roots the way excavation does, but we still work around drip lines and root flare rather than through them, on a property where the tree is often older than the house.</p>` },
      { h2: `A town that's been rebuilt before`, html: `<p>Hurricane Hazel destroyed the original community cabana in 1954, and Briarcliffe Acres has carried a documented storm history ever since on a stretch of coast with an 84 percent annual chance of some tropical-cyclone impact. What a storm leaves on turf here is salt film and blown debris under the canopy rather than open sand, and the cleanup is the same either way: debris cleared, a fresh-water flush, edges and seams checked. <a href="/services/storm-and-seasonal-cleanup/">Storm and seasonal cleanup</a>.</p>` },
    ],
